02:47:27.180 --> 02:47:32.880
The helicopter hovered over the backstreets of&nbsp;
Manhattan. To the untrained eye, it would look&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:32.880 --> 02:47:37.740
like any commercial or news helicopter, the&nbsp;
kind of thing that might catch your attention&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:37.740 --> 02:47:43.800
for a moment and then leave just as quickly, as&nbsp;
your mind wanders over to wondering what you’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:43.800 --> 02:47:49.859
have for dinner. Nobody would know from a mere&nbsp;
glance that Mobile Task Force Iota-5 were inside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:49.859 --> 02:47:55.979
a four man team on a dangerous mission.&nbsp;
This has always been the greatest power&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:55.979 --> 02:48:03.479
of the SCP Foundation: Hiding in plain sight,&nbsp;
using the mundane as a cloak to go unnoticed.

02:48:03.479 --> 02:48:10.439
But this time, the monster they were hunting was&nbsp;
capable of doing the exact same thing. And for&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:10.439 --> 02:48:17.219
the personnel of the Foundation, this ability is&nbsp;
an affectation, a learned and adopted skill. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:17.220 --> 02:48:26.100
SCP-247, this ability - that it employs to deadly&nbsp;
effect - comes as naturally as breathing air.

02:48:26.100 --> 02:48:30.060
The helicopter was monitoring local&nbsp;
police scanners and phone activity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:30.060 --> 02:48:35.640
as well as receiving direct radio orders from&nbsp;
Command back at a classified containment site.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:35.640 --> 02:48:41.100
The latest intel was incoherent, horrified&nbsp;
screaming over the phone, traced back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:41.100 --> 02:48:48.840
a nearby alleyway. Someone had seen something&nbsp;
horrifying. This was Iota-5’s cue to intervene.

02:48:48.840 --> 02:48:53.220
They rappelled down from the helicopter&nbsp;
onto the roof of a nearby tenement building,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:53.220 --> 02:49:00.120
clad in thick tactical armor, antimemetic&nbsp;
SCRAMBLE goggles, DAN-INJECT IM injection&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:00.120 --> 02:49:07.019
rifles loaded with 10 cc S10 Syringe Darts - each&nbsp;
one carrying an immobilizing payload of potent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:07.020 --> 02:49:12.240
fast-acting xylazine - and high-powered&nbsp;
conventional weaponry in case of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:12.240 --> 02:49:19.319
emergencies. If anyone in the world was capable&nbsp;
of tracking, securing, and containing SCP-247,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:19.319 --> 02:49:26.639
it was these four operatives. And yet, by the end&nbsp;
of today’s mission, one of them would be dead.

02:49:26.640 --> 02:49:32.340
But they didn’t know that yet. Like all good MTF&nbsp;
members, they had tunnel vision for the mission&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:32.340 --> 02:49:37.380
at hand. They used a fire escape ladder on the&nbsp;
side of the building to reach the bottom of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:37.380 --> 02:49:44.100
alleyway quickly. There, they came upon a gruesome&nbsp;
sight: The aftermath of an attack. On the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:44.100 --> 02:49:49.380
a dead body with what looked like claw marks&nbsp;
all over it. Iota-5 found another civilian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:49.380 --> 02:49:53.640
half-mad with confusion and terror,&nbsp;
hiding behind a nearby dumpster.

02:49:53.640 --> 02:49:59.819
She just kept shaking and repeating, “But&nbsp;
she was so little” over and over again.

02:49:59.819 --> 02:50:04.799
One member of the task force, Corporal&nbsp;
Rico, elected to stay behind and secure&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:04.800 --> 02:50:09.420
the area while Foundation cleanup teams&nbsp;
zeroed in to sweep the scene and provide&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:09.420 --> 02:50:14.520
amnestics to witnesses. The remaining three&nbsp;
continued the high-stakes chase on foot.

02:50:14.520 --> 02:50:20.280
Their eyes in the sky radioed in, “Stay frosty,&nbsp;
Iota, we’ve got unusual incident reports from an&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:20.280 --> 02:50:24.179
apartment building a quarter-klick North&nbsp;
of your position. Potential fatalities;&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:24.180 --> 02:50:29.220
247 is in an extremely hostile&nbsp;
state. Engage with caution. Over.”

02:50:29.220 --> 02:50:33.960
The trio engaged quickly, running towards&nbsp;
the source of the disturbance at break-neck&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:33.960 --> 02:50:37.920
speeds. They knew they were getting&nbsp;
closer when they heard the shrieking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:37.920 --> 02:50:44.460
and the deep, guttural growls. That low,&nbsp;
primal rumbling of a true apex predator.

02:50:44.460 --> 02:50:50.040
Already, this containment breach was turning&nbsp;
out to be a horrific mess. It was going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:50.040 --> 02:50:54.180
a nightmare for the higher-ups at the clean&nbsp;
up and misinformation departments to handle:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:54.180 --> 02:50:59.819
Paying to repair inexplicable property damage,&nbsp;
providing amnestics to tens if not hundreds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:59.819 --> 02:51:04.380
traumatized witnesses, creating plausible&nbsp;
cover stories for upwards of at least ten&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:04.380 --> 02:51:08.880
dead New Yorkers. But even all that mess&nbsp;
would have to wait until the creature&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:08.880 --> 02:51:15.240
was actually captured and contained once&nbsp;
more, which would be an ordeal unto itself.

02:51:15.240 --> 02:51:20.219
They found SCP-247’s access point&nbsp;
into the building: A wooden door,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:20.220 --> 02:51:25.319
torn to splinters by huge claws&nbsp;
and fangs. Iota-5 charged inside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:25.319 --> 02:51:31.380
Injection Rifles at the ready, only to find&nbsp;
carnage in the hallway. Three more bodies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:31.380 --> 02:51:38.040
with deep claw marks cutting into their flesh. It&nbsp;
was a harrowing sight, but the team didn’t have&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:38.040 --> 02:51:45.359
time to waste processing it. They needed to stop&nbsp;
247 before it killed again, and again, and again…

02:51:45.359 --> 02:51:50.700
The team followed the trail of blood, and the&nbsp;
claw marks carved into the ground where 247&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:50.700 --> 02:51:55.260
had passed. After killing the three people&nbsp;
in the hallway, it’d destroyed another door&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:55.260 --> 02:52:01.859
and gained access to a nearby stairwell. The&nbsp;
blood continued up the steps. Iota-5 pursued.

02:52:01.859 --> 02:52:07.019
The next splintered door, clawed and bashed off&nbsp;
its hinges, led them further. They could hear&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:07.020 --> 02:52:13.560
the growling again. Distant, but clearly audible.&nbsp;
They were close. There were another two attacked&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:13.560 --> 02:52:18.479
corpses in the hallway. Previous corpses&nbsp;
had been partially or even entirely eaten,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:18.479 --> 02:52:24.599
these two had just been clawed to death. Defensive&nbsp;
kills. They’d backed the beast into a corner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:24.600 --> 02:52:29.220
and now it was panicking, slaughtering&nbsp;
anything it perceived as an interference.

02:52:29.220 --> 02:52:32.580
They pressed on now. This was the end game.

02:52:32.580 --> 02:52:37.800
Soon, they found the final busted door;&nbsp;
this one led into one of the building's&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:37.800 --> 02:52:44.760
private apartments. There’d be no other way&nbsp;
out from here. 247 was trapped. The three&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:44.760 --> 02:52:50.279
members of Iota-5 shouldered their Injection&nbsp;
Rifles, and crept inside, full stealth mode.

02:52:50.279 --> 02:52:56.099
Something about the apartment was haunting. It&nbsp;
portrayed all the signs of belonging to a young,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:56.100 --> 02:53:00.540
single mother and her only son. This&nbsp;
was confirmed when they found the dead&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:00.540 --> 02:53:05.640
body of the mother in the living room, eyes&nbsp;
glassy and throat clawed out with one feral&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:05.640 --> 02:53:10.979
strike. They could hear something else in&nbsp;
the apartment, too. A soft, gentle cooing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:10.979 --> 02:53:16.500
too human to be anything else. They followed the&nbsp;
noise until they reached the child’s bedroom.

02:53:16.500 --> 02:53:22.500
What they saw there caused even the most hardened&nbsp;
member of the trio to break out in a cold sweat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:22.500 --> 02:53:29.160
This was the one thing they didn’t want to&nbsp;
happen. The child, utterly oblivious to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:29.160 --> 02:53:34.380
atrocities that had unfolded in his building&nbsp;
- to even the death of his own mother - was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:34.380 --> 02:53:40.620
sitting in his bedroom, petting a kitten. It&nbsp;
was the sweetest little cat you’ve ever seen:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:40.620 --> 02:53:48.720
A fluffy, harmless creature with an orange,&nbsp;
striped coat. This is the dreaded SCP-247.

02:53:48.720 --> 02:53:54.420
The child just kept repeating, “Good&nbsp;
kitty, good kitty…” while petting her.

02:53:54.420 --> 02:54:00.540
SCP-247 purred and rolled around playfully.&nbsp;
To the untrained eye, it would seem that a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:00.540 --> 02:54:04.680
kitty like this couldn’t hurt a fly.&nbsp;
But that’s where you couldn’t be more&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:04.680 --> 02:54:09.359
wrong. Iota-5 stared at this display&nbsp;
in a state of nerve-shredding terror,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:09.359 --> 02:54:12.899
thinking fast, trying to&nbsp;
calculate their next move.

02:54:12.899 --> 02:54:17.879
“Aw, what an adorable little cat!” one&nbsp;
of the Iota-5 members found themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:17.880 --> 02:54:22.020
blurting out. It was clear that 247’s&nbsp;
effects were starting to have an impact&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:22.020 --> 02:54:26.220
on even these hardened soldiers,&nbsp;
and they needed to act quickly.

02:54:26.220 --> 02:54:31.500
They could fire a tranquilizer dart right&nbsp;
here, right now, but in the ensuing panic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:31.500 --> 02:54:38.700
SCP-247 would almost definitely lash out and&nbsp;
maul the child. It wasn’t a risk they could take,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:38.700 --> 02:54:43.920
but time was already running out. 247&nbsp;
would only allow the child to play for&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:43.920 --> 02:54:51.120
so long before something terrible happened. It&nbsp;
was an inevitable part of SCP-247’s process.

02:54:51.120 --> 02:54:55.200
That’s when Private Kowalski, one&nbsp;
of the remaining members of Iota-5,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:55.200 --> 02:54:58.979
stepped forward. He was about to&nbsp;
do the only thing he could do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:58.979 --> 02:55:05.279
knowing it would cost him his life. As he took&nbsp;
his final action, the Foundation credo looped&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:05.279 --> 02:55:11.700
once more through his head, “We die in the&nbsp;
dark so that others can live in the light.”

02:55:11.700 --> 02:55:15.179
Fighting against the urge to rush&nbsp;
forward and start petting the cat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:15.180 --> 02:55:20.460
he instead lunged forward and struck the cat with&nbsp;
his foot. Immediately, it turned and pounced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:20.460 --> 02:55:26.580
knocking Kowalski against the wall with terrifying&nbsp;
force. It gave the most ferocious growls as it&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:26.580 --> 02:55:31.920
tore into the screaming Kowalski, eviscerating&nbsp;
his abdomen. The child screamed and retreated&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:31.920 --> 02:55:38.040
into the opposite corner of the room. Kowalski was&nbsp;
done for, but now, his teammates had a clear shot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:38.040 --> 02:55:44.160
They secured 247 in their sights, and unloaded&nbsp;
several darts into its flank, sedating it with&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:44.160 --> 02:55:50.340
their powerful tranquilizers. Soon after, it gave&nbsp;
a lethargic growl, and collapsed onto the ground.

02:55:50.340 --> 02:55:55.740
An evac and containment team was already&nbsp;
on the way, along with the cleaners. Many&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:55.740 --> 02:56:01.319
had died that day, but at least SCP-247&nbsp;
would be brought back into containment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:01.319 --> 02:56:07.139
preventing further bloodshed. Kowalski would&nbsp;
be given a posthumous medal of bravery for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:07.140 --> 02:56:12.180
selfless actions in the line of duty, as well&nbsp;
as a generous stipend to his grieving family.

02:56:12.180 --> 02:56:17.160
By this point, you’re probably wondering: How&nbsp;
can a harmless kitten cause so much carnage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:17.160 --> 02:56:24.359
mayhem, and despair? The answer is simple: It’s&nbsp;
not a kitten at all, it’s a fully-grown female&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:24.359 --> 02:56:31.920
Bengal Tiger, nine feet and 400 pounds of pure&nbsp;
muscle, with four inch claws, four inch canines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:31.920 --> 02:56:36.899
and an inbuilt killer instinct. But&nbsp;
due to an anomalous memetic effect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:36.899 --> 02:56:43.859
247 can only ever be perceived as a sweet,&nbsp;
tiny housecat. But even worse, due to its&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:43.859 --> 02:56:50.040
additional cognitohazardous properties, almost&nbsp;
everyone who sees 247 is compelled to approach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:50.040 --> 02:56:55.680
and begin doting and fawning over it.&nbsp;
And that’s exactly how it gets its prey.

02:56:55.680 --> 02:57:01.080
Its memetic properties are actually so flawless&nbsp;
that, regardless of Foundation countermeasures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:01.080 --> 02:57:06.540
it has never been perceived as anything other&nbsp;
than a kitten. It’s actually only through forensic&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:06.540 --> 02:57:12.300
analysis into things like bite and claw marks left&nbsp;
on victims, measurements of weight, and abnormal&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:12.300 --> 02:57:17.160
water displacement in aqueous environments&nbsp;
that we can tell it’s really a tiger at all.

02:57:17.160 --> 02:57:23.340
For a brief period, SCP-148 - a metal&nbsp;
that seems to counteract cognitohazards&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:23.340 --> 02:57:28.800
and mind-warping anomalies - was used in the&nbsp;
containment of this Euclid-class creature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:28.800 --> 02:57:34.680
but these measures were abandoned after SCP-148&nbsp;
proved to have a negative impact on nearby&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:34.680 --> 02:57:40.920
Foundation staff after prolonged exposure. Now,&nbsp;
strict protocols of surveillance and distancing&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:40.920 --> 02:57:47.580
are required to prevent hapless Foundation staff&nbsp;
members from being lured to their doom by 247.

02:57:47.580 --> 02:57:54.660
The head researcher on the SCP-247 case wanted&nbsp;
to conduct a series of experiments on SCP-247,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:54.660 --> 02:58:00.000
hoping to find more about the interplay of its&nbsp;
unique traits with other members of the animal&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:00.000 --> 02:58:06.120
kingdom. Two control animals were brought&nbsp;
in, to represent the duality of SCP-247:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:06.120 --> 02:58:12.120
Control A was a yellow kitten, roughly&nbsp;
matching 247’s apparent shape and size.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:12.120 --> 02:58:18.300
Control B was a fully-grown Bengal Tiger,&nbsp;
representing 247’s actual shape and size.

02:58:18.300 --> 02:58:23.760
A mixed-breed, cat-chasing terrier was brought&nbsp;
in for the first test. It immediately followed&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:23.760 --> 02:58:28.680
Control A around, barking wildly until&nbsp;
it retreated up a tree. Unsurprisingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:28.680 --> 02:58:34.500
it fled in terror from Control B, cowering in&nbsp;
the corner. Control B paid no mind to the dog,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:34.500 --> 02:58:42.479
perhaps considering it beneath its attention,&nbsp;
even as prey. With SCP-247, the dog initially ran&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:42.479 --> 02:58:48.479
towards it, barking, until it was only five meters&nbsp;
away. At this point, 247 let out an irritated&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:48.479 --> 02:58:55.200
mewling noise, and the dog fled to the corner&nbsp;
in terror, suddenly aware of its true nature.

02:58:55.200 --> 02:58:59.939
For test number two, a male tabby&nbsp;
kitten was brought in. As expected,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:59.939 --> 02:59:04.979
it simply played with Control A, and&nbsp;
was terrified of Control B. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:04.979 --> 02:59:10.439
first part of the test with 247, the anomalous&nbsp;
attributes of the creature led to some surreal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:10.439 --> 02:59:15.839
mind-bending footage. At multiple&nbsp;
points, 247 picked up the other kitten,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:15.840 --> 02:59:22.020
as an adult female tiger might do with a cub,&nbsp;
and lifted it higher than 247 seemed to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:22.020 --> 02:59:27.660
capable of. It was like watching a glitch&nbsp;
in real time. In a second part of the test,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:27.660 --> 02:59:33.840
the kitten immediately fled in terror,&nbsp;
suggesting 247 can control how it’s perceived.

02:59:33.840 --> 02:59:40.439
Next came a deer, prey typical of a Bengal&nbsp;
Tiger. Neither Control B nor 247 were fed&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:40.439 --> 02:59:45.660
for three days prior to this experiment,&nbsp;
and both killed and ate the deer. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:45.660 --> 02:59:51.359
curiously, 247 killed the deer with a single,&nbsp;
merciful bite to the neck before eating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:51.359 --> 02:59:56.399
compared to its more brutal methods with&nbsp;
human targets. And when this experiment&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:56.399 --> 03:00:01.679
was repeated with 247 after it hadn’t been&nbsp;
starved, it showed little interest in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:01.680 --> 03:00:08.220
deer until it became hungry again, suggesting&nbsp;
it inherently prefers human or human-like prey.

03:00:08.220 --> 03:00:13.080
This theory was further reinforced during&nbsp;
tests with a chimpanzee. The chimp fell&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:13.080 --> 03:00:18.180
under 247’s cognitohazardous spell, and&nbsp;
began to pet it. Seven minutes later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:18.180 --> 03:00:23.580
247 attacked and brutally devoured&nbsp;
the chimp, as it would its human prey.

03:00:23.580 --> 03:00:28.620
But strangely, the most frightening result&nbsp;
of all came when SCP-247 was paired with&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:28.620 --> 03:00:34.559
an adult male Bengal Tiger. They engaged in&nbsp;
standard mating behavior, which later led to&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:34.560 --> 03:00:41.880
the duo reproducing a new creature - designated&nbsp;
SCP-247-1 - which had all the anomalous traits&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:41.880 --> 03:00:48.899
of SCP-247. But the frightening realizations&nbsp;
don’t stop there. Studies into genetic material&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:48.899 --> 03:00:55.799
provided by SCP-247 shows slight deviation from&nbsp;
a typical Bengal Tiger's genotype, suggesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:55.800 --> 03:01:01.020
contamination from another creature that mated&nbsp;
with one of its non-anomalous tiger parents.

03:01:01.020 --> 03:01:05.160
For context, tiger broods&nbsp;
have an average of three cubs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:05.160 --> 03:01:09.779
but can have as many as seven - a&nbsp;
common practice, given how tiger cubs&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:09.779 --> 03:01:14.399
are vulnerable to cannibalism from rival&nbsp;
adult males looking to court the females.

03:01:14.399 --> 03:01:21.359
This leads to only one frightening conclusion:&nbsp;
There could be more creatures just like SCP-247&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:21.359 --> 03:01:26.880
out there, apex predators that seem to the&nbsp;
untrained eye like cute, harmless kittens.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:26.880 --> 03:01:32.040
They could be anywhere, and if they’re close&nbsp;
enough to each other or other non-anomalous&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:32.040 --> 03:01:37.439
tigers, they could breed even further,&nbsp;
becoming the scariest invasive urban&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:37.439 --> 03:01:43.799
species you’ve ever seen. Because, after all,&nbsp;
we know what their preferred prey is now.

03:01:43.800 --> 03:01:49.500
So next time you see a cute little orange&nbsp;
tabby cat and feel the instinct to pet it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:49.500 --> 03:01:54.540
take a second to think about it. It&nbsp;
may be the last thing you ever do.

03:01:54.540 --> 03:01:59.040
Did you hear that not so long ago,&nbsp;
a five-year-old boy went up against&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:59.040 --> 03:02:04.740
twelve of Russia’s greatest chess grandmasters&nbsp;
- And do you know what happened? He lost every&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:04.740 --> 03:02:08.639
single match. That’s because winning&nbsp;
at chess, dear viewers, isn’t easy.&nbsp;

03:02:08.640 --> 03:02:12.000
Chess is an ancient game of strategy, cunning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:12.000 --> 03:02:17.279
and skill. It’s not just about thinking one&nbsp;
step ahead of your opponent, that’s not going&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:17.279 --> 03:02:22.319
to be enough. Make a wrong prediction and you&nbsp;
could end up sacrificing one of your pieces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:22.319 --> 03:02:27.059
as well as vital space on the board.&nbsp;
But thinking two to three steps ahead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:27.060 --> 03:02:33.359
beating your adversary’s moves before they’ve even&nbsp;
been made. Now there’s a viable strategy. Tricky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:33.359 --> 03:02:39.359
but viable; after all, if it was easy, we’d&nbsp;
all be Queen’s Gambit level chess prodigies.&nbsp;

03:02:39.359 --> 03:02:45.000
Eric Matthews had never been good at the game,&nbsp;
but he had a pretty substantial reason to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:45.000 --> 03:02:50.939
trying. And that reason’s name was Brian&nbsp;
Matthews, his father. You see, for as long&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:50.939 --> 03:02:55.859
as Eric could remember, his dad had regarded&nbsp;
a high level of skill on the chessboard to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:55.859 --> 03:03:01.559
a sign of intellectual superiority. Intelligence&nbsp;
was something that Brian put quite a considerable&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:01.560 --> 03:03:06.840
value on, given that he was a professor at a&nbsp;
university in their home county in England.&nbsp;

03:03:06.840 --> 03:03:12.600
Some of Eric’s earliest memories were of playing&nbsp;
chess with his dad, usually on a Friday night when&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:12.600 --> 03:03:18.060
Brian got home after a week of giving lectures to&nbsp;
the next generation of scholars. Obviously, with&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:18.060 --> 03:03:23.880
his son at such a young age, the professor would&nbsp;
take it easy on Eric, playing in a much laxer&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:23.880 --> 03:03:29.939
fashion, focusing instead on teaching the boy&nbsp;
the basics of the game. And, for a time at least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:29.939 --> 03:03:36.059
it was good. It was a rare time that Eric and his&nbsp;
academic father could spend bonding, after all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:36.060 --> 03:03:42.000
with his mother gone, his dad was all he had.
But as the years passed, the game changed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:42.000 --> 03:03:46.560
By the time Eric was a teenager, Brian&nbsp;
had stopped pulling his punches on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:46.560 --> 03:03:50.819
chessboard. He had hoped his son would build on&nbsp;
what he’d learned when they played in the past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:50.819 --> 03:03:55.979
using those skills to best his dad on the&nbsp;
board. But to Eric, playing chess had never&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:55.979 --> 03:04:01.559
been about a purely educational experience; it&nbsp;
was more about spending time with his old man.&nbsp;

03:04:01.560 --> 03:04:07.620
Time after time, the young man’s pawns fell&nbsp;
prey to Brian’s expertly considered and far more&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:07.620 --> 03:04:14.460
competitive moves. Try as he might, Eric couldn’t&nbsp;
best his dad. He tried his best, and never stopped&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:14.460 --> 03:04:19.800
putting the effort into every game, but thinking&nbsp;
too hard about one possible plan of attack left&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:19.800 --> 03:04:25.380
him wide open to a counter-strategy from the&nbsp;
professor. Over and over again he landed himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:25.380 --> 03:04:31.140
in checkmate, or made illegal moves without&nbsp;
even realizing it, every time earning criticism&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:31.140 --> 03:04:36.660
and chastisement from his scholarly father.
Every game it got worse, it was like Eric&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:36.660 --> 03:04:41.939
could feel his father’s gaze and the weighty&nbsp;
expectations behind it with each move he made&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:41.939 --> 03:04:46.379
across the board. There were so many nights&nbsp;
where he wondered if it would be better to&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:46.380 --> 03:04:51.120
give up entirely, to knock down his own&nbsp;
king and concede. But how could he ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:51.120 --> 03:04:55.439
find any other shared interests with his&nbsp;
dad beyond the two of them playing chess?&nbsp;

03:04:55.439 --> 03:05:01.379
It had become a lifeline, tethering father&nbsp;
and son together, and to cut it now left&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:01.380 --> 03:05:07.800
Eric uncertain if he’d sink or be able to swim&nbsp;
alongside Brian. He had long admired his father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:07.800 --> 03:05:13.200
his achievements in academia were impossible&nbsp;
to avoid, with more framed certificates hung&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:13.200 --> 03:05:16.859
up on the walls than there were photos of&nbsp;
the pair of them together. But the shadow&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:16.859 --> 03:05:21.779
it cast over him made Eric desperate to&nbsp;
keep this one shred of common ground alive.&nbsp;

03:05:21.779 --> 03:05:27.420
Eric wasn’t the type to give up, despite how much&nbsp;
of an uphill struggle the situation felt like.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:27.420 --> 03:05:32.760
Taking a leaf out of his professor father’s&nbsp;
scholarly ways, he decided to learn the game&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:32.760 --> 03:05:39.720
inside and out, every known move and strategy. He&nbsp;
would research the entire history of chess itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:39.720 --> 03:05:44.340
if that’s what it took to play with&nbsp;
the same skill as his dear old dad.&nbsp;

03:05:44.340 --> 03:05:49.319
Over the coming weeks, Eric checked out every&nbsp;
book at his local library on the subject;&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:49.319 --> 03:05:55.259
Beginner’s Guides, Advanced Rulebooks, and even&nbsp;
a few volumes on notable players throughout the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:55.260 --> 03:05:59.880
extensively long history of the game.&nbsp;
Along the way, a chapter of a certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:59.880 --> 03:06:05.399
book stood out to Eric. It described a chess&nbsp;
prodigy from Russia, who had created an early&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:06:05.399 --> 03:06:11.519
mechanical chess device known as ‘The Samurai’.
It had been designed to be a traveling curiosity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:06:11.520 --> 03:06:16.560
and would sit playing chess games against&nbsp;
volunteers taken from a spectating audience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:06:16.560 --> 03:06:21.779
each one of them having forked over some of their&nbsp;
hard-earned money to watch this man-made wonder.&nbsp;

03:06:21.779 --> 03:06:27.660
The Russian chess prodigy’s young daughters also&nbsp;
had a love for the game. Seeing that gave Eric a&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:06:27.660 --> 03:06:32.399
pang of jealousy, wondering if those daughters&nbsp;
had as much trouble playing their own father&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:06:32.399 --> 03:06:37.799
as he did with Brian. But at least there was&nbsp;
an underlying shred of hope there too, if this&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:06:37.800 --> 03:06:42.840
father and his daughters could bond over chess,&nbsp;
maybe there was a chance for Eric and his dad too.&nbsp;

03:06:42.840 --> 03:06:48.060
Sadly, it’s one thing to try and learn&nbsp;
all the facts you can about chess. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:06:48.060 --> 03:06:52.319
an entirely different beast to put all&nbsp;
that information to use and apply it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:06:52.319 --> 03:06:57.840
an actual game. Despite having read every book&nbsp;
he could get his hands on, Eric still couldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:06:57.840 --> 03:07:02.939
best Brian at the board. It was like nothing&nbsp;
had changed, his father barely noticed when&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:07:02.939 --> 03:07:07.859
Eric tried to replicate move sets he’d read&nbsp;
up on, and still managed to not only counter&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:07:07.859 --> 03:07:13.799
those moves but check his queen in the process.
So, practice, Eric thought. After all, practice&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:07:13.800 --> 03:07:18.660
was supposedly meant to make perfect, right?&nbsp;
The plan was simple if he practiced his chess&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:07:18.660 --> 03:07:23.880
moves enough times, and figured out how he could&nbsp;
call on what he’d learned, then he might stand a&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:07:23.880 --> 03:07:29.279
chance at winning when he and his dad played each&nbsp;
other. There was just one hiccup to the plan:&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:07:29.279 --> 03:07:35.580
Eric needed someone to practice against. The only&nbsp;
other person in the house was Brian, meaning it&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:07:35.580 --> 03:07:41.279
was that hiccup that turned into a problem almost&nbsp;
big enough to stop Eric in his tracks. That is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:07:41.279 --> 03:07:45.779
until he went into his father’s lab.
It was under the house itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:07:45.779 --> 03:07:52.139
a sort of sub-level, maybe used as a basement&nbsp;
or cellar by the previous owners. But since&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:07:52.140 --> 03:07:57.420
Professor Matthews and his son had lived there,&nbsp;
the entire room had been remodeled into an at-home&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:07:57.420 --> 03:08:02.760
laboratory. Not a terribly advanced one, of&nbsp;
course, this was the early nineties, after&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:08:02.760 --> 03:08:07.380
all. A majority of Brian’s time, even when he&nbsp;
was at home instead of working at the university,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:08:07.380 --> 03:08:13.680
was spent on his own, downstairs in the lab.
Eric had gone down there in search of his dad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:08:13.680 --> 03:08:18.420
to ask him if there was anyone whom he knew who&nbsp;
he could practice and develop his chess skills&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:08:18.420 --> 03:08:24.300
with. But, instead, what he found down there&nbsp;
was the last thing he expected to see. Not that&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:08:24.300 --> 03:08:31.620
he had any clue exactly what it was, at first.
The… thing was some kind of bizarre contraption,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:08:31.620 --> 03:08:36.960
a collection of components that didn’t seem&nbsp;
to be in any logical configuration. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:08:36.960 --> 03:08:43.319
it was primarily comprised of something that Eric&nbsp;
recognized all too well: a chess table. This one&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:08:43.319 --> 03:08:49.319
was metal, steel to be precise, and seemed to&nbsp;
be hooked up to some sort of computer. While,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:08:49.319 --> 03:08:54.120
back in the nineties, computers were hardly as&nbsp;
commonplace as they are now, Eric had seen a&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:08:54.120 --> 03:09:01.319
fair few at school and the library. Although&nbsp;
this one was different, it seemed… old. Far&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:09:01.319 --> 03:09:05.580
older than Eric thought computers had been&nbsp;
around for; as far as he knew, they’d only&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:09:05.580 --> 03:09:10.920
really come to prominence in the mid-eighties.
But this computer looked like it pre-dated even&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:09:10.920 --> 03:09:15.540
that period. Noticing another part of the&nbsp;
contraption – a large steam engine with&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:09:15.540 --> 03:09:21.720
the words ‘Manufactured by Maudslay, Sons&nbsp;
&amp; Field, established 1840’ engraved on one&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:09:21.720 --> 03:09:26.580
side – made it seem that this whole device&nbsp;
had been around since the Victorian era.&nbsp;

03:09:26.580 --> 03:09:32.460
The next part that caught his eye was the chess&nbsp;
pieces themselves, each one standing neatly in&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:09:32.460 --> 03:09:37.859
its place on the board. They looked delicate,&nbsp;
intricately carved from some smooth substance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:09:37.859 --> 03:09:42.299
For a moment, Eric toyed with the thought&nbsp;
of how they could even be made from bone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:09:42.300 --> 03:09:48.779
noting how each pawn, knight, rook, bishop,&nbsp;
king and queen were all about the size of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:09:48.779 --> 03:09:53.580
human finger bone. He dismissed the idea;&nbsp;
nobody would ever do something like that.&nbsp;

03:09:57.000 --> 03:10:02.220
Eric grabbed a sheet covering a larger component&nbsp;
hooked up to the mess. Lifting it away in a swift&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:10:02.220 --> 03:10:09.600
pull, it unveiled what was sitting beneath: a full&nbsp;
suit of 18th Century Samurai armor. Eric looked&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:10:09.600 --> 03:10:14.220
closer at the embellishments on the surface of&nbsp;
the pauldrons; he was no expert on Feudal Japan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:10:14.220 --> 03:10:19.560
but it looked authentic enough to be the real&nbsp;
thing, if not a very close approximation.&nbsp;

03:10:19.560 --> 03:10:23.880
Taking a look at the collection of oddities&nbsp;
all tethered together in his father’s lab,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:10:23.880 --> 03:10:30.120
a certain detail of all his chess research came&nbsp;
to the forefront of Eric’s mind. The armor had&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:10:30.120 --> 03:10:36.960
given it away: this was ‘The Samurai’ – or, at&nbsp;
the very least, a least a crude, homemade version&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:10:36.960 --> 03:10:42.779
of it that his dad had put together. But if it&nbsp;
worked, it was also something to practice against.&nbsp;

03:10:42.779 --> 03:10:48.240
It didn’t take Eric long to start tinkering with&nbsp;
the contraption, trying to get it to work. All&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:10:48.240 --> 03:10:52.620
the while, the question of why his dad owned&nbsp;
such a thing kept drumming up noise in the back&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:10:52.620 --> 03:10:58.920
of his head. Had Brian built it, or was this the&nbsp;
original made by that Russian chess prodigy? Was&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:10:58.920 --> 03:11:04.140
this machine the reason that Eric’s dad possessed&nbsp;
such an unbeatable skill at chess? And would using&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:11:04.140 --> 03:11:09.300
it give him the edge he needed to best him&nbsp;
at the game and earn his father’s respect?&nbsp;

03:11:09.300 --> 03:11:14.700
After what felt like hours upon hours of trial and&nbsp;
error with a machine he could barely comprehend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:11:14.700 --> 03:11:19.740
Eric seemed to have cracked it. As far as&nbsp;
he could tell, the steam engine powered the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:11:19.740 --> 03:11:24.719
whole contraption and could be set to five&nbsp;
different speeds, labeled on the side in&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:11:24.720 --> 03:11:28.979
roman numerals. The power from the engine&nbsp;
was then fed to some kind of sophisticated&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:11:28.979 --> 03:11:34.979
mechanism that was within the suit of samurai&nbsp;
armor, allowing it to move, and what appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:11:34.979 --> 03:11:39.779
to be a series of electromagnets that moved&nbsp;
the chess pieces and kept them on the board.&nbsp;

03:11:39.779 --> 03:11:45.359
Flicking it onto the third-highest of the five&nbsp;
speed levels, the machine whirred into life;&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:11:45.359 --> 03:11:50.339
the sounds of creaking, and grinding of&nbsp;
metal filled the lab. Kneeling opposite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:11:50.340 --> 03:11:55.920
Eric went to make the first move… only to stop&nbsp;
himself. It wasn’t that he’d changed his mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:11:55.920 --> 03:12:01.800
about practicing against the Samurai, but because&nbsp;
of the speed he’d set it to. Determining that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:12:01.800 --> 03:12:05.880
settings must have correlated to difficulty&nbsp;
levels, Eric figured that if he really wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:12:05.880 --> 03:12:11.460
to get the most rigorous practice, to really&nbsp;
hone his chess moves, he needed to commit fully.&nbsp;

03:12:11.460 --> 03:12:16.680
Reaching for the dial, he turned the device up&nbsp;
to its fifth and highest speed, then made his&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:12:16.680 --> 03:12:21.660
first move. He pushed one of the bone-colored&nbsp;
pawns forward by a single square and waited.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:12:21.660 --> 03:12:27.720
A split second passed, and the arm of the early&nbsp;
automaton responded with its first countermove. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:12:27.720 --> 03:12:32.700
was quick, almost moving with the same natural&nbsp;
fluidity and speed as an actual human being;&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:12:32.700 --> 03:12:38.399
albeit still with a little bit of creaking,&nbsp;
and some slight, clockwork-like stutters. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:12:38.399 --> 03:12:43.620
it worked nonetheless, the machine could play.
The tension over the first game was palpable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:12:43.620 --> 03:12:48.840
forming a layer of sweat over Eric’s forehead.&nbsp;
Every whirr and tick of the machine gave the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:12:48.840 --> 03:12:53.939
impression they were playing with a stop clock&nbsp;
timing each of their moves, adding to the urgency.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:12:53.939 --> 03:13:01.259
Despite this, Eric Matthews tried his best to&nbsp;
stay calm. This was practice after all, a dry run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:13:01.260 --> 03:13:06.960
not the inevitable game he’d play against his dad.
With every move he made, his heart drummed against&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:13:06.960 --> 03:13:13.080
his ribs, uncertain he’d made the right call. Each&nbsp;
time the robotic hand cruelly knocked one of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:13:13.080 --> 03:13:19.080
pieces away, Eric felt the surge of frustration,&nbsp;
but told himself to quell it. He kept focused,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:13:19.080 --> 03:13:25.080
used what he’d researched to adapt and respond&nbsp;
accordingly to each of the machine’s moves, until…&nbsp;

03:13:25.080 --> 03:13:27.720
Checkmate.
He’d beaten it – he&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:13:27.720 --> 03:13:33.899
might have lost everything save for a knight, a&nbsp;
rook, his king and queen – but he had won. Trapped&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:13:33.899 --> 03:13:38.460
without anywhere else to move on the board, the&nbsp;
metal finger of the automaton conceded the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:13:38.460 --> 03:13:44.160
knocking over its own king in resignation.&nbsp;
Panting, heart racing from the sheer excitement&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:13:44.160 --> 03:13:49.080
of being on the winning side of a game, Eric&nbsp;
hurriedly gathered up and reset all the pieces.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:13:49.080 --> 03:13:54.660
He had to go again, not just so he could be&nbsp;
certain it wasn’t a fluke, but to make sure he&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:13:54.660 --> 03:13:59.760
had what it took to take on his professor father.
Back and forth Eric went with the chess machine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:13:59.760 --> 03:14:05.640
over and over again. They were fairly evenly&nbsp;
matched, it seemed. Eric won the second game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:14:05.640 --> 03:14:10.920
only to be best on the next two. But it was&nbsp;
some time afterward – he had lost count of&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:14:10.920 --> 03:14:15.899
exactly how many rounds later – that things&nbsp;
started to change. Maybe it was the age and&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:14:15.899 --> 03:14:21.179
condition of the Victorian era chess computer, the&nbsp;
natural wear and tear stopping it from functioning&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:14:21.180 --> 03:14:25.620
properly. But Eric noticed the Samurai&nbsp;
started to make moves that were illogical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:14:25.620 --> 03:14:30.479
that practically offered him the upper hand&nbsp;
with no discardable strategy behind them.&nbsp;

03:14:30.479 --> 03:14:36.120
Then, its movements became flat-out illegal,&nbsp;
disregarding the directions and number of squares&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:14:36.120 --> 03:14:41.460
each different piece was allowed to move. Before&nbsp;
long, it was moving them erratically around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:14:41.460 --> 03:14:46.439
chessboard, refusing to cooperate and forcing&nbsp;
Eric to call an end to the day’s practicing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:14:46.439 --> 03:14:52.019
Unfortunately, it wouldn’t be the end of the&nbsp;
unusual things that would happen that day.

03:14:52.020 --> 03:14:58.439
“Did you send me that weird email?” was the first&nbsp;
question Eric’s dad asked him when he returned&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:14:58.439 --> 03:15:03.179
home, looking noticeably under the weather.
Confused, Eric said he had no idea what his&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:15:03.180 --> 03:15:07.620
father was talking about. Professor Matthews&nbsp;
then went on to describe what he’d received on&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:15:07.620 --> 03:15:13.319
his work computer. It had been an email, with a&nbsp;
file attached to it named ‘shakhmaty’, a word in&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:15:13.319 --> 03:15:18.899
Russian that translates to ‘chess’. Embedded in&nbsp;
the email below – although it had taken a long&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:15:18.899 --> 03:15:24.359
time to load being opened on a 1990s computer&nbsp;
– there had apparently been a photo as well.&nbsp;

03:15:24.359 --> 03:15:30.599
“It was rather odd, Eric,” Brian went on. “Quite&nbsp;
unnerving to tell the truth. Black and white,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:15:30.600 --> 03:15:35.399
all sort of distorted and stretched.&nbsp;
But it looked like two young girls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:15:35.399 --> 03:15:42.779
one grinning and the other screaming. I’ve been&nbsp;
feeling… well, not quite myself since I saw it.”&nbsp;

03:15:42.779 --> 03:15:47.099
With that, Brian excused himself, stating&nbsp;
he’d been suffering from headaches and a high&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:15:47.100 --> 03:15:52.260
temperature, and as a result, needed to go and&nbsp;
lay down. It wasn’t like his father to get ill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:15:52.260 --> 03:15:56.340
Eric thought, but of course, he had no&nbsp;
reason to assume it was anything serious.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:15:56.340 --> 03:16:00.420
Probably just a spate of fatigue after a&nbsp;
long day of teaching at the university,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:16:00.420 --> 03:16:07.260
no cause for concern. If only that’s all it was.
Within a few hours, Brian was completely restless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:16:07.260 --> 03:16:12.720
so unable to sleep that simply taking a nap&nbsp;
was impossible. He kept calling to Eric,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:16:12.720 --> 03:16:17.760
complaining of the sound of childlike laughter&nbsp;
coming from somewhere in the house, but his son&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:16:17.760 --> 03:16:23.520
hadn’t heard anything. By the time the sun went&nbsp;
down, Eric was trying to calm his dad down through&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:16:23.520 --> 03:16:28.260
a rush of intense anxiety that gripped him.
Brian had claimed to be hallucinating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:16:28.260 --> 03:16:32.220
seeing the warped faces of two girls&nbsp;
that frightened him half to death.&nbsp;

03:16:32.220 --> 03:16:37.439
It was getting late, long past the time they&nbsp;
usually played chess together, but for now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:16:37.439 --> 03:16:42.779
Eric’s mind was focused solely on helping his&nbsp;
dad. For a while, he seemed to be able to calm his&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:16:42.779 --> 03:16:49.380
father down, only to realize Brian wasn’t settled&nbsp;
at all. He was awake, eyes open, fully conscious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:16:49.380 --> 03:16:54.600
but wasn’t responding at all to Eric asking&nbsp;
if he was alright. Instead, the accomplished&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:16:54.600 --> 03:16:59.580
academic just stared blankly into space.
Eric had been up all night, exhausted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:16:59.580 --> 03:17:03.960
worried for Brian’s safety, and completely&nbsp;
clueless about what was happening to him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:17:03.960 --> 03:17:08.580
After a while of being non-responsive, his&nbsp;
dad seemed to regain a little bit of lucidity&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:17:08.580 --> 03:17:13.920
once more, but his behavior was erratic.
“Take me back to work, I need to get on my&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:17:13.920 --> 03:17:18.180
computer!” Brian demanded of his son.
When Eric refused, that’s when his&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:17:18.180 --> 03:17:21.840
dad got angry, and agitated.
Professor Brian Matthews was&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:17:21.840 --> 03:17:27.240
sadly found dead within the next few months.
Several months later, as the sole executor of&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:17:27.240 --> 03:17:32.399
his father’s estate and last living relative, Eric&nbsp;
had to be the one to go through his dad’s personal&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:17:32.399 --> 03:17:39.719
belongings. Volumes upon volumes of thick academic&nbsp;
books, his smart, scholarly clothes. The house was&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:17:39.720 --> 03:17:44.939
almost clear now, save for one thing that was left&nbsp;
in the basement lab. Flicking on the light switch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:17:44.939 --> 03:17:50.519
Eric looked at the Samurai sitting motionless,&nbsp;
still uncovered after their last practice game.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:17:50.520 --> 03:17:55.740
He’d sold off the chess pieces to a collector&nbsp;
in New York, now it was just an empty oddity.&nbsp;

03:17:55.740 --> 03:18:00.540
Eric placed a king on the board, one of the&nbsp;
ones that he and his dad had used when they&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:18:00.540 --> 03:18:05.580
played each other. With a gentle flick of his&nbsp;
finger, he toppled it, resigning to the strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:18:05.580 --> 03:18:11.160
automaton and leaving it there in the laboratory.&nbsp;
He wasn’t sure how, but he had some kind of gut&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:18:11.160 --> 03:18:16.380
feeling that this chess machine had somehow been&nbsp;
responsible for his father’s fate. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:18:16.380 --> 03:18:24.420
Eric had no idea what the device actually was, and&nbsp;
what… or who lay beneath its whirring metal parts.&nbsp;

03:18:24.420 --> 03:18:31.020
SCP-1875 is the designation given to this&nbsp;
machine now, and has been ever since the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:18:31.020 --> 03:18:36.000
Foundation recovered it not long after Eric sold&nbsp;
his father’s house. They were able to learn much&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:18:36.000 --> 03:18:42.120
more about it than young Mr. Matthews, or even the&nbsp;
late Professor Matthews, ever had. Not just how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:18:42.120 --> 03:18:48.240
make it work or what its function was, but who&nbsp;
had built it… and who had been used to build it.&nbsp;

03:18:48.240 --> 03:18:52.080
Although details of his real name&nbsp;
eluded the Foundation’s top researchers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:18:52.080 --> 03:18:56.939
they were able to uncover newspaper articles&nbsp;
about the device from all over Russia, America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:18:56.939 --> 03:19:02.460
and England, dating back to the early&nbsp;
1900s. The Russian chess prodigy who’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:19:02.460 --> 03:19:06.600
invented it and toured the chess-playing&nbsp;
machine around, had used some interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:19:06.600 --> 03:19:11.279
components to make his automaton. Maybe they&nbsp;
were where it derived its skill at the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:19:11.279 --> 03:19:16.439
and its apparent temperament when the device&nbsp;
was made to play at maximum speed for too long.&nbsp;

03:19:16.439 --> 03:19:22.919
He had used his daughters to make the machine.
Deep within the heart of this early form of&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:19:22.920 --> 03:19:28.560
computer, the two girls brain tissue had been&nbsp;
hooked up to the electromagnets. The machine’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:19:28.560 --> 03:19:33.840
moves were theirs; each pawn or rook shifting&nbsp;
across the black and white squares of the board,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:19:33.840 --> 03:19:38.460
every rule or strategy their prodigy&nbsp;
father had taught, it all determined&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:19:38.460 --> 03:19:43.200
how the device played. Their minds, taken&nbsp;
from their skulls, were now the analytical&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:19:43.200 --> 03:19:49.380
engine of their father’s creation. The bones of&nbsp;
their fingers, he'd carved into chess pieces.&nbsp;

03:19:49.380 --> 03:19:56.160
Over the coming weeks, the Foundation naturally&nbsp;
ran their usual slew of tests on SCP-1875,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:19:56.160 --> 03:20:02.220
playing multiple games of chess against it. Every&nbsp;
time, they increased the machine’s five levels of&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:20:02.220 --> 03:20:07.439
speed, until it started behaving erratically&nbsp;
while on the highest setting. Shortly after,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:20:07.439 --> 03:20:14.399
every member of personnel working on SCP-1875&nbsp;
received a bizarre email. It contained a file,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:20:14.399 --> 03:20:20.219
named with the Russian word for chess…
And a photograph; of the stretched, distorted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:20:20.220 --> 03:20:27.660
smiling, and screaming faces of two young&nbsp;
girls. Faces that you might be seeing very soon…

